Subject: [PATCH 3/3] STEREO3D.md: Where the pages that set a wallpaper read
 its cost

KWin publishes each output's wallpaper cut on org.kde.KWin.StereoDepth,
and the pages that set a wallpaper state that number.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
---
 STEREO3D.md | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/STEREO3D.md b/STEREO3D.md
index 871fbae..9f4991b 100644
--- a/STEREO3D.md
+++ b/STEREO3D.md
@@ -152,7 +152,7 @@ With the eyes swapped, the picture is the right eye's and the parallax runs the
 
 **Three places, declared: sunk, screen and popped.** All subtle, a few pixels, with the limits the user's own settings (System Settings, "3D Depth": how far popped comes out and sunk goes back, in pixels at 1920 wide, scaled to each screen). The active window sits at the screen; its menus, tooltips and notifications pop; the wallpaper and the windows behind sink.
 
-**The wallpaper is the deepest layer, on the lock screen too.** The wallpaper lies at the sunk limit, behind every window, and so does the lock screen's wallpaper, while the lock screen's clock and password field stay at the screen. The wallpaper's copy moves in each eye and keeps its size: nothing is stretched, since KWin scales once, at the output. The columns either eye's copy leaves open are cut from both sides, the same columns in both eyes: half the level's parallax per side, rounded up, from the user's Background depth and each output's own width (5 columns at 1920 wide with the default of 10, 4 at 1280). The wallpaper's own fill mode still lays the picture over the whole screen, so nothing else is missing, and with a Background depth of 0 nothing is cut. On the lock screen the wallpaper is what the greeter places below its own surface, as the layer of a stereo video wallpaper is; a 2D wallpaper that the greeter draws into its own surface, with the interface, stays at the screen until the greeter gives it a surface of its own.
+**The wallpaper is the deepest layer, on the lock screen too.** The wallpaper lies at the sunk limit, behind every window, and so does the lock screen's wallpaper, while the lock screen's clock and password field stay at the screen. The wallpaper's copy moves in each eye and keeps its size: nothing is stretched, since KWin scales once, at the output. The columns either eye's copy leaves open are cut from both sides, the same columns in both eyes: half the level's parallax per side, rounded up, from the user's Background depth and each output's own width (5 columns at 1920 wide with the default of 10, 4 at 1280). The wallpaper's own fill mode still lays the picture over the whole screen, so nothing else is missing, and with a Background depth of 0 nothing is cut. KWin publishes what each output's wallpaper loses on the D-Bus, `org.kde.KWin.StereoDepth` at `/org/kde/KWin/StereoDepth`: `WallpaperCuts` gives each output, by name, the columns cut at each side now, 0 on an output in 2D, and `WallpaperCutsAt` the same for a Background depth not applied yet. The pages that set a wallpaper (the desktop's, System Settings' Wallpaper and the lock screen's) and the 3D Depth page state that number in the user's words, so the cost is never a fixed 10 and never a formula of their own. On the lock screen the wallpaper is what the greeter places below its own surface, as the layer of a stereo video wallpaper is; a 2D wallpaper that the greeter draws into its own surface, with the interface, stays at the screen until the greeter gives it a surface of its own.
 
 **Everything in between is KWin's job, by stacking order.** Several stacked background windows fan out from sunk up through the screen towards popped, always behind the active window, in the same order as in 2D. A window caught in the middle of a pile, its edges out of the stack, then reads as a sheet floating within it, the way a pile of papers looks in real life. No program declares this; it falls out of the order.
 
